WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.031 --> 00:00:04.990
One of the most common questions I see in
the comments of these videos is,

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:08.150
“Where do I start my microscopy journey?

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:12.645
What kind of microscope should I get if I'm
just starting out?”

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:16.881
Well, that is the very question we were trying
to answer when we created

00:00:16.881 --> 00:00:19.480
the Microcosmos Microscopes.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:24.500
Developed in partnership with our master of
microscopes, James Weiss, they are a fantastic

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:30.000
entry level microscope and it is available
with either plan achromatic objectives or

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.820
regular achromatic objectives over at 
microcosmos.store/microscope.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:36.820
And you might be thinking,

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:41.260
“I don't know what an objective is, and
I sure don't know what a plan objective is

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:44.044
or why you want to upgrade your microscope
with them.”

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:49.149
Well, we have a bunch of information about
that over there as well, and also a bunch

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:52.745
of videos about how to use your microscope
when it arrives.

00:00:52.745 --> 00:00:57.057
Also, if you previously purchased a Microcosmos
microscope and would like to upgrade your

00:00:57.057 --> 00:01:02.489
objectives, we have a four pack of plan objectives
for sale separately as well as brand new 20x

00:01:02.489 --> 00:01:07.000
and 60x plan objectives that can be purchased
individually.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.112
You can get all of that and more at Microcosmos.store.

00:01:36.355 --> 00:01:41.282
Our master of microscopes, James, was not
in fact always a master.

00:01:41.282 --> 00:01:46.290
Like everyone else, he had to start somewhere,
and that “somewhere” was a microscopy

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:50.594
lab course where he saw a scene similar to
this one.

00:01:54.803 --> 00:02:00.192
There were thousands and thousands of teeny
tiny things that he didn’t know well enough

00:02:00.192 --> 00:02:01.516
to identify.

00:02:01.516 --> 00:02:06.969
And these minuscule unknown creatures were
behaving oddly.

00:02:06.969 --> 00:02:11.970
They were gathering together on the slide
so tightly that it looked like they were trapped

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.290
by invisible walls.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:19.040
Any time one of them tried to swim away and
escape those walls, it would quickly reverse

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:23.720
its direction and swim right back, like you're 
leaving the house, and then you realize

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:28.140
that it's raining and cold and 
immediately turn back inside.

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:33.120
In that comparison, you are making an active
decision to turn back.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:40.170
But for James, new to the wondrous world of
microbes, it was hard to imagine why the creatures

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:41.720
would act like that.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:46.520
So he asked his instructor what was going
on, but the teacher just said he was

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:50.438
looking at some bacteria and didn’t elaborate.

00:02:50.438 --> 00:02:53.510
As you can imagine, this was not a very helpful
answer.

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:57.040
But it wasn’t completely un-helpful either.

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:58.500
After all, before this,

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:04.346
James had just imagined microbes as amorphous
blobs that sit around and get you sick.

00:03:04.346 --> 00:03:08.238
But seeing something like this quickly shatters
that image.

00:03:08.238 --> 00:03:14.239
It makes bacteria and the other residents
of the microcosmos seem so much more alive

00:03:14.239 --> 00:03:19.335
and aware than you’d expect,
from you know, a blob.

00:03:19.335 --> 00:03:23.430
Well that’s because microbes are not just blobs.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:30.358
They are very well-evolved biological machinery,
the product of eons of evolution that have

00:03:30.358 --> 00:03:36.939
exposed their ancestors and them to different
homes and foods and threats.

00:03:36.939 --> 00:03:43.230
The machine that James was watching in action
there,  was the flagellum, a long helical filament

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:49.319
that either drives the bacteria towards an
area or tumbles it away based on the chemical

00:03:49.319 --> 00:03:51.651
it senses in the environment.

00:03:51.651 --> 00:03:56.720
The bacteria does this with proteins on its
surface, which then send a signal through

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:00.149
the cell to control the flagellar motor.

00:04:00.149 --> 00:04:06.180
This motor then controls the movement of the
flagella, which controls the movement of the cell.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:13.560
And as we watch more complex organisms, we
can see a similar behavior, like this Euplotes.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:19.300
It feels wrong to even compare them to a machine
because they seem so cognizant of everything

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:25.131
around them, even without access to all of
the senses that we have available to us.

00:04:25.131 --> 00:04:28.720
But they don't have those senses,
because they don’t need them.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:34.930
These microbes that we are watching have
the same underlying capacity to assemble behavior

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:39.233
out of proteins and ions and signals that
our bodies do.

00:04:39.233 --> 00:04:46.160
In fact, our senses and awareness are built
with those same fundamental pieces, they're just

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:51.090
assembled on different scales of size
and time, and complexity.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:53.748
So even though the pieces are always same,

00:04:53.748 --> 00:04:58.696
the scale of  behavior and decisions 
are vast when you consider all

00:04:58.696 --> 00:05:05.423
the organisms out there and all the choices
they must be facing every single day.

00:05:05.423 --> 00:05:12.500
But we journeyers though the microcosmos are
fortunate that scientists have been very curious

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:18.139
about a few species in particular and the
choices they seem capable of.

00:05:18.139 --> 00:05:23.670
One species that has been particularly interesting
to learn from is our favorite trumpet-shaped

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:26.044
protists, the Stentors.

00:05:27.894 --> 00:05:32.026
Stentors have a kind of odd problem for the
microcosmos:

00:05:32.026 --> 00:05:34.577
they are very big.

00:05:34.577 --> 00:05:42.280
So big that they have to contend with non-microcosmos
predators, like fish and worms and insect larvae.

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:47.190
To deal with that challenge, Stentor coeruleus
has an ingenious solution:

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:50.898
if it touches something, it curls up in a
ball.

00:05:50.898 --> 00:05:52.189
It’s a great solution.

00:05:52.189 --> 00:05:53.016
Lovely.

00:05:53.016 --> 00:05:54.403
Even a little bit cute.

00:05:54.403 --> 00:05:59.328
If a threat approaches you, curl up into
a ball, and what can it really do to you then?

00:05:59.328 --> 00:06:01.200
Except that there’s a problem.

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:02.610
A bunch of them really.

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:07.251
When you’re living in a pond surrounded
by other organisms that aren’t fish, how

00:06:07.251 --> 00:06:12.106
do you survive if you’re constantly curling
up into yourself?

00:06:12.106 --> 00:06:14.127
Wouldn’t that get tiring?

00:06:14.127 --> 00:06:19.130
We can see our stentors here are mostly relaxed,
so how do they do that?

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:23.102
How do they figure out what contact is non-threatening?

00:06:23.102 --> 00:06:26.830
They learn through a process called
habituation.

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:30.980
If something touches the Stentor, it will
contract away from the force.

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:35.960
But if that force happens again and again
and again, and especially if the force is

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:41.539
not that strong, the stentor becomes less
and less likely to contract.

00:06:41.539 --> 00:06:46.373
And stentors are able to remember this for
several hours.

00:06:46.373 --> 00:06:52.360
But as much as scientists have watched Stentors
learn and remember, the mechanism that makes

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:56.553
them capable of this is still mysterious to
us.

00:06:56.553 --> 00:07:01.240
Another organism that scientists love to watch
learn is slime mold.

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:06.930
In one set of experiments, scientists watched
slime mold move towards food through different

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:09.400
temperature and humidity conditions.

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:15.083
Once an hour, the scientists would expose
the slime to cold, dry air, during which the

00:07:15.083 --> 00:07:17.291
slime mold would slow down.

00:07:17.291 --> 00:07:21.979
When they were done with the cold exposure
and returned to moist, warm conditions, the

00:07:21.979 --> 00:07:24.379
slime mold would pick its speed back up

00:07:24.379 --> 00:07:30.123
The scientists repeated this three times,
and then they left the slime molds alone,

00:07:30.123 --> 00:07:34.150
keeping them in the warm temperatures that
the organisms prefer.

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:39.268
But even though the scientists were no longer
switching things back to cold, the slime molds

00:07:39.268 --> 00:07:46.187
would, an hour later, slow down again as if
they remembered what had happened and expected

00:07:46.187 --> 00:07:48.162
it to get cold.

00:07:48.162 --> 00:07:52.641
Some would even do it again two and 
even three hours later.

00:07:52.641 --> 00:07:59.770
Like Stentors, we don’t know the machines
that make this memory and behavior possible.

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:06.909
But we are of course drawn to that mystery,
like bacteria drawn towards an invisible gradient,

00:08:06.909 --> 00:08:12.582
we return again and again to the question
that we have not yet answered.

00:08:12.582 --> 00:08:18.740
Can these organisms think and remember? 
What does it mean to think at all? 

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:20.823
Are these organisms just machines?

00:08:20.823 --> 00:08:27.922
And if so, are we just machines? The result 
of chemical reactions that we do not control.

00:08:27.922 --> 00:08:30.789
Or are we something more?

00:08:30.789 --> 00:08:35.589
The answers to that probably can not be found 
in the microcosmos alone,

00:08:35.589 --> 00:08:38.590
but that is no reason not to look.

00:08:45.888 --> 00:08:52.501
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:08:52.501 --> 00:08:56.450
And if you are interested in exploring that
world on your own, I will remind you there's

00:08:56.450 --> 00:09:01.470
a link in the description to the Microcosmos
Microscope, a microscope we made specifically

00:09:01.470 --> 00:09:03.959
for people who are starting off on their journey.

00:09:03.959 --> 00:09:08.670
It can be a little bit complicated and intimidating
at first, but that microscope comes along

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:13.140
with a few things to help you on your journey,
including a mount for your phone so you can

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:17.980
video whatever you're seeing, and also a series
of videos that we made to help you get the

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:20.110
best out of your microscope.

00:09:21.532 --> 00:09:24.640
The people on the screen right now are our
Patreon patrons.

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:30.850
They're a bunch of people who think that there
should be weird, chill content about microscopic

00:09:30.850 --> 00:09:32.913
organisms on YouTube.

00:09:32.913 --> 00:09:36.980
Obviously, we agree with them, but we could
not do this on our own.

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:42.650
So we are so grateful to all of the people
who help us out at Pareon.com/JourneytoMicro.

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:47.019
If you'd like to see your name up here, that
is the place to go.

00:09:47.019 --> 00:09:49.600
If you want to see more from our master of
microscopes, James Weiss.

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:52.388
You can check out Jam and Germs on Instagram.

00:09:52.388 --> 00:09:57.553
And if you want to see more from us, there's
probably a subscribe button somewhere nearby.

